item.text:
**Gap Status**
- Project-level outcome audits: **partly resolved**
- Personnel replacement or corrective adaptation after failure: **partly resolved**, but direct evidence of replacing a specific ineffective implementer is still **not recoverable** from the public 2022 record

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"1875 Projects are on Project Monitoring Group (PMG) Portal as on 28.07.2022","publisher":"Press Information Bureau, Government of India","publication_date":"2022-08-03","url":"https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleasePage.aspx?PRID=1847860&lang=2&reg=3","claim":"As of 28.07.2022, 1,875 infrastructure projects were on the PMG portal; the government said it had resolved 5,119 issues pertaining to 1,095 projects, while 634 projects still showed time overruns due to land acquisition, forest clearance, right of way, right of use, and court cases.","locator":"headline and body text of the release","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous official monitoring statistics with concrete counts and named bottleneck categories.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and directly about project execution status.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but material: the PMG system operated under Modi's government and reflects executive monitoring capacity; the delays also reflect state-level and legal constraints.","contrary_evidence":["The same release reports substantial unresolved time overruns, so it is mixed rather than purely positive."],"lenses":["8B.6","8B.7","8B.8","8B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Shri Piyush Goyal reviews progress of PM GatiShakti National Master Plan","publisher":"Press Information Bureau, Government of India","publication_date":"2022-11-16","url":"https://www.pib.gov.in/Pressreleaseshare.aspx?PRID=1876607&reg=48&lang=2","claim":"The review said PM GatiShakti had incorporated more than 1,900 GIS data layers, that over 250 infrastructure projects had been examined in the last year, and that the plan had expedited many previously stalled projects.","locator":"paragraphs 13-18 of the release","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Specific contemporaneous government progress report with quantitative implementation details.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and squarely about implementation progress in the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but relevant to Modi's program: this is follow-through on a flagship mechanism launched under his government, though the review itself is ministerial rather than a personal PM act.","contrary_evidence":["It remains a government self-report and does not independently audit project completion quality or durability."],"lenses":["8B.3","8B.5","8B.6","8B.8","8B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Grievance redressal timeline reduced from 45 days to 30 days","publisher":"Press Information Bureau, Government of India / Ministry of Personnel, Public Grievances & Pensions","publication_date":"2022-07-31","url":"https://www.pib.gov.in/Pressreleaseshare.aspx?PRID=1846752&lang=2&reg=48","claim":"The government reduced the public-grievance redressal timeline from 45 days to 30 days, describing the change as consistent with Prime Minister Modi's emphasis on effective grievance disposal.","locator":"lead paragraph and quoted ministerial statement in the PIB release","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct government policy change with a specific timeline adjustment and explicit linkage to the PM's stated administrative priority.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and relevant to administrative correction and follow-through.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but material: the policy sits within Modi's governance style and administration, but it is not itself evidence that he personally replaced underperforming staff.","contrary_evidence":["A shorter target is a process reform, not proof that service quality or outcomes fully improved."],"lenses":["8B.8","8B.9","8B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Union Minister Dr Jitendra Singh says, a Task Force has been constituted to coordinate the progress of filling up of the vacant posts as per the 'Recruitment Plan'","publisher":"Press Information Bureau, Government of India / Ministry of Personnel, Public Grievances & Pensions","publication_date":"2022-07-14","url":"https://www.pib.gov.in/newsite/PrintRelease.aspx?relid=235197","claim":"A task force was constituted to coordinate filling vacant posts under the recruitment plan after the Prime Minister's instruction to recruit 10 lakh people in mission mode, and CPGRAMS was made operational in all scheduled languages.","locator":"headline and first two paragraphs of the PIB release","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Specific staffing and process response tied to a direct PM instruction, with clear 2022 timing.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and directly about staffing adaptation in response to a PM-directed target.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable at the level of executive direction: the PM set the recruitment target, and the ministry created a coordination mechanism to implement it; it still does not prove replacement of any specific ineffective official.","contrary_evidence":["The release shows staffing correction, but not evidence that the government replaced a particular failed implementer."],"lenses":["8B.4","8B.8","8B.9","8B.10"]}

**What changed**
- The project-monitoring gap is now better covered: there is direct 2022 evidence of monitored projects, resolved issues, remaining overruns, and PM GatiShakti project examination.
- The adaptation gap is now better covered: there is direct 2022 evidence of grievance-timeline shortening and a staffed task force for recruitment-plan execution.
- The one part still not recovered is a clean public record of Modi replacing a specific ineffective implementer because of a missed target.
